<article class="post hentry">
<a href="/en/blog-details.html">
</a>
<header class="entry-header">
</header>
<div class="entry-content">
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/csgv1.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Nguồn: soha.vn</div>
</div>
<h3>Introductory Article:</h3>
<h4>• Value</h4>
<p>
    Located in the heart of Ho Chi Minh City, the Weapon Bunker used in the attack on the Independence Palace was one of the urban commando bases established in preparation for the Tet Offensive of 1968.<br/>
Today, the weapon bunker has been recognized as a National Historical Site, serving as a powerful reminder of history that attracts thousands of visitors, both locals and foreigners.<br/>
It stands as a symbol of the bravery and resilience of the Saigon Special Forces (Biệt động Sài Gòn) during the resistance war against American imperialism for national liberation.
  </p>
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/csgv2.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Nguồn: vnexpress.vn</div>
</div>
<h4>• Origins – Structure – Ways of transporting, hiding weapons</h4>
<p>
    Following orders from the Saigon–Gia Định Military Region Command, the combat-support units of the Saigon Special Forces built numerous secret facilities to sustain long-term operations.<br/>
From 1966, comrade Trần Văn Lai (alias Mai Hồng Quế), an officer of the Saigon Special Forces, selected and purchased the house at 287/70 Phan Đình Phùng Street (now Nguyễn Đình Chiểu Street) to carry out the unit’s plan: the construction of a secret underground bunker.<br/>
Mr. and Mrs. Năm Lai risked their own lives and their family’s safety to achieve the feat of digging a tunnel right in the heart of enemy territory. Under the house floor, inside a room, there are two adjacent bunkers. Each bunker has a large crawl-through drain that opens to the back of the house.<br/>
The weapon bunker measures 2 m long × 1.2 m wide × 2.5 m high; its walls and floor were rendered with a thick layer of cement to make them watertight. The bunker opening is covered by a lid made from six bricks bonded together. The lid has a screw-type latch and a ring on the latch so that, when lifted, there is just enough space for a person to slip down.<br/>
By mid-1967 the bunker was completed and reported to the Support Unit (coded J9 T700). After inspection, the unit commander ordered the plan to proceed.<br/>
To transport a large quantity and variety of weapons (handguns, rifles, AK ammunition, B40 rockets, explosives, etc.) to the secret bunker, the Support Unit prepared a set of hollow wooden crates made by Mr. Trần Phú Cương (alias Năm Mộc), a city-based Special Forces craftsman. Once the crates were finished, they were taken to Phước Hiệp commune (near Bàu Mây – Suối Cụt), where the weapons were hidden inside the crates.<br/>
Mr. Chín Teng (a revolutionary contact) then used a bullock cart to move them to a rendezvous point in Củ Chi. Disguised as a commercial transaction, the weapon-filled crates were transferred onto a truck bearing license plate EC6045, driven by operative Ba Bảo (Nguyễn Văn Bảo). On another occasion weapons were concealed inside a cà tăng (a type of circular woven container with coir walls).<br/>
Each time the truck delivered weapons to the house, the men chose dusk or night, backing the vehicle deep into the yard to unload so as to avoid attracting attention.
  </p>
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/csgv3.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Nguồn: cafebiz.vn</div>
</div>
<h4>• The Course of the Attack on the Independence Palace – Tết Mậu Thân 1968</h4>
<p>
    At 1:30 a.m. on January 31, 1968 (the night between the first and second day of the Lunar New Year, Tết Mậu Thân), Team 5 of the Saigon Special Forces (Unit 345), consisting of 15 officers and soldiers led by Comrade Trương Hoàng Thanh, received their weapons from the secret bunker and departed to attack the Independence Palace — the headquarters of the puppet government.<br/>
At 1:45 a.m., the entire team, traveling in three small cars, approached the side gate of the Independence Palace on Nguyễn Du Street. The assault group used explosives to destroy the gate, but the charge failed to detonate. Several soldiers rushed into the compound but were met with heavy gunfire. The enemy quickly countered, blocking the assault direction, forcing the team to engage in open combat on Nguyễn Du Street.<br/>
By dawn, seven comrades had fallen, including Team Leader Trương Hoàng Thanh. The remaining eight fighters, half of them wounded, withdrew and took refuge on the third floor of house No. 56 Thủ Khoa Huân Street. There, they bravely held their position throughout January 31, repelling multiple counterattacks by enemy forces.<br/>
At 3:00 a.m. on February 1, the seven surviving soldiers, exhausted and out of ammunition, moved to house No. 108 Gia Long Street (now Lý Tự Trọng Street). Surrounded and unable to continue fighting, they were captured by the enemy.<br/>
Battle results: Team 5 destroyed three Jeep vehicles, killed or wounded nearly 100 enemies (including several Americans). On the Vietnamese side, eight comrades were killed, four were wounded, and seven were captured.<br/>
For their extraordinary bravery, Team 5 of the Saigon Special Forces was awarded the Third-Class Military Exploit Order and later honored as Heroes of the People’s Armed Forces on December 20, 1969.<br/>
Four days after the battle, the enemy discovered the secret base at 287/70 Phan Đình Phùng Street and arrested Comrade Trần Văn Lai. His house and belongings were confiscated and occupied by the enemy until April 30, 1975.


  </p>
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/csgv4.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Nguồn: trip.com</div>
</div>
<h4>• Visiting and Exploration Activities</h4>
<p>
    A visit to this historical site is far from boring — the tour “Following the Footsteps of the Saigon Special Forces” will take you to various historical locations associated with the activities of the Saigon Commandos during the resistance war against the U.S. <br/>
Through this journey, visitors not only have the chance to discover and experience historical values told by living witnesses, but also to gain a deeper understanding of the history, culture, and patriotic spirit of the people of old Saigon. <br/><br/>
Tour Operators:<br/>
Vietravel – Vietnam Travel and Marketing Transport JSC: https://travel.com.vn
<br/>
Saigontourist – One-Member Tourism Services Co., Ltd.: https://www.saigontourist.net
<br/>
Fiditour Travel JSC: https://www.fiditour.com
<br/>
Nam Trip Travel Co., Ltd.: https://namtriptravel.com/tour-biet-dong-sai-gon.html
<br/>
KKday: https://www.kkday.com/vi/product/121259
<br/>
Klook: https://www.klook.com/vi/activity/81428-biet-dong-sai-gon-half-day-tour/
  </p>
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/csgv5.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Nguồn: soha.vn</div>
</div>
